      "title": "Strategy heuristic: leads and running-total traps",
      "kind": "strategy_atom",
      "edition_ids": ["two-player-standard-en"],
      "topics": ["strategy", "play", "lead"],
      "summary": "Advisory only. Leading a 5 is dangerous because any 10-value card makes 15 for the opponent. Leading a 4 is comparatively safe because it can't be immediately raised to 15 with a single card. Leading from a pair is often good if the matching partner is still in hand. Aces and 2s are usually held to help hit 15, 31, or salvage a Go. Try not to leave the running total sitting at 21 for the opponent — a 10-value response makes 31.",
